---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SPF Softfail vs Fail: ~all vs -all Explained"
  description="SPF softfail (~all) vs fail (-all): what each qualifier means, how receivers decide what to do, and how to choose based on your verified sender inventory and forwarding risk."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SPF Softfail vs Fail</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SPF Softfail vs Fail: <code class="font-mono">~all</code> vs <code class="font-mono">-all</code></h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      The difference between <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">~all</code> and <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">-all</code> is what your published record tells the evaluator about unauthorized senders. This page explains what each qualifier actually does — and what it does not.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Terminology note</h2>
      <p>
        The SPF result for <code class="font-mono">-all</code> is <strong class="text-ink">fail</strong> (the term "hardfail" is commonly used but is not the RFC-defined result name). The result for <code class="font-mono">~all</code> is <strong class="text-ink">softfail</strong>. This page uses both common terms and the RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§2.6</a> result names.
      </p>

      <h2>What the qualifiers do</h2>
      <p>
        <code class="font-mono">~all</code> and <code class="font-mono">-all</code> determine the SPF <em>result</em> when evaluation reaches the <code class="font-mono">all</code> mechanism and no earlier mechanism has matched the connecting IP. The result is an assertion in your published policy:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><code class="font-mono">~all</code> → result is <strong class="text-warn">softfail</strong>: "a weak statement that the host is probably not authorized" (RFC 7208 §2.6.5)</li>
        <li><code class="font-mono">-all</code> → result is <strong class="text-fail">fail</strong>: "an explicit statement that the client is not authorized" (RFC 7208 §2.6.4)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        What the receiver <em>does</em> with that result is a separate decision. Receiving systems apply their own disposition policy, which may consider SPF along with DKIM, DMARC, content, reputation, engagement, and other signals.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th><code class="font-mono">~all</code> (softfail)</th><th><code class="font-mono">-all</code> (fail)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">SPF result</td><td class="text-sm text-warn">softfail</td><td class="text-sm text-fail">fail</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">What your policy asserts</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The host is probably not authorized (weak statement)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The host is not authorized (explicit statement)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">What the receiver does</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver's own disposition policy</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiver's own disposition policy</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink-faint">Neither result specifies or guarantees what the receiver will do. Source: RFC 7208 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§2.6</a>.</p>

      <h2>What neither qualifier guarantees</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><code class="font-mono">-all</code> does <strong class="text-ink">not</strong> guarantee rejection. A receiver may reject, quarantine, accept, or ignore the SPF fail result entirely, depending on their filtering policy.</li>
        <li><code class="font-mono">~all</code> does <strong class="text-ink">not</strong> guarantee delivery. A receiver may still spam-folder the mail based on other signals, or treat softfail as a significant negative.</li>
        <li>Neither qualifier provides <strong class="text-ink">guaranteed spoofing protection</strong>. A spoofer's mail may still be delivered depending on the receiver's filtering decisions.</li>
        <li>An SPF policy checker — including ours — <strong class="text-ink">cannot</strong> establish inbox placement or predict what any specific receiver will do. It reports what your published policy says.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Forwarding and DKIM interaction</h2>
      <p>
        Forwarding commonly causes SPF problems: when a message passes through a forwarder, the forwarder's IP is typically not authorized by the original sender's SPF record, producing an SPF fail result at the final recipient — regardless of whether the original sender used <code class="font-mono">~all</code> or <code class="font-mono">-all</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In forwarding scenarios, an <strong class="text-ink">aligned DKIM pass</strong> can allow DMARC to pass (see <a href="/dmarc-alignment/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC alignment explained</a>), provided the DKIM signature survives any modifications made during forwarding. However, DKIM passing does <strong class="text-ink">not</strong> change the SPF result — an SPF fail remains an SPF fail. The two mechanisms are independent.
      </p>

      <h2>Is <code class="font-mono">-all</code> required for DMARC?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-ink">No.</strong> DMARC does not require <code class="font-mono">-all</code>. DMARC evaluates whether an <em>aligned</em> SPF pass or DKIM pass exists (see <a href="/dmarc-alignment/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC alignment explained</a>). A message can pass DMARC with <code class="font-mono">~all</code> as long as the sender is listed and the domain aligns. Conversely, a message from an unlisted sender with <code class="font-mono">-all</code> will fail SPF — but if DKIM is aligned and passes, DMARC can still pass.
      </p>
      <p>
        Switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code> does not improve DMARC alignment and is not a prerequisite for DMARC enforcement.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        The choice should be based on your <strong class="text-ink">verified sender inventory</strong> and <strong class="text-ink">operational risk</strong>, not on a mandatory progression from softfail to fail.
      </p>

      <h3>Consider <code class="font-mono">~all</code> when:</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>You are not confident every legitimate sender is listed (new services, departments adding tools, third-party integrations).</li>
        <li>Your mail is frequently forwarded (forwarders typically cause SPF fail regardless of qualifier).</li>
        <li>You are still building your sender inventory and need a safety margin.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Consider <code class="font-mono">-all</code> when:</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>You have verified every legitimate sender through DMARC reports and/or test messages over time.</li>
        <li>All senders are listed in your SPF or have aligned DKIM signatures.</li>
        <li>You understand that any future unlisted sender will produce an SPF fail result.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Coverage limitations of sender verification</h3>
      <p>
        DMARC aggregate reports show senders that mailbox providers observed over the reporting period. Test messages show what you explicitly tested. Neither method guarantees discovery of <em>every</em> legitimate sender: a sender that has not sent recently may not appear in reports, and a sender you have not tested will not appear in test results. Before switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code>, consider whether there are senders you may have missed (legacy systems, backup procedures, new tools being evaluated by other teams).
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Switching to <code class="font-mono">-all</code> before verifying senders:</strong> legitimate mail may start producing SPF fail results, potentially including password resets and order confirmations.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Treating <code class="font-mono">-all</code> as a DMARC requirement:</strong> it is not. DMARC can pass with <code class="font-mono">~all</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Assuming <code class="font-mono">-all</code> blocks all spoofing:</strong> receivers decide what to do with an SPF fail result.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Forgetting forwarding:</strong> forwarded mail typically fails SPF regardless of qualifier. Ensure DKIM is aligned so DMARC can pass independently.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul class="grid gap-2">
        <li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records: the complete guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc-alignment/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC alignment explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF Record Checker (free tool)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC: the complete guide</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Primary reference: <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-2.6" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">RFC 7208 §2.6 (Result Definitions)</a> and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7208#section-4.6.2" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§4.6.2 (Qualifiers)</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
